WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.680
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant, a problem solving website

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.750
that teaches you think like an engineer.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:11.719
If you have been on the internet in the past
month, you have probably seen a picture of

00:00:11.719 --> 00:00:13.400
Elon Musk’s latest project.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.410
A rocket that looks like the brainchild of
a H. G Well’s fever dream of the future.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:21.789
It doesn’t look like any current generation
rocket by any shape or measure.

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:26.429
It’s shorter and fatter than your typical
Space X rocket, and most strange of all, it’s

00:00:26.429 --> 00:00:28.029
made of stainless steel.

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:33.040
A material that has largely fallen out of
use for propellant tanks since the 60s.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.350
Steel is strong, but it’s pretty heavy.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.160
Making it unsuitable for flight structures.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.379
Reducing the weight of the launch vehicle
is an art form in rocket science.

00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:46.500
Every kilogram matters, and engineers have
come up with some innovative ways to reduce

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.500
weight.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.399
WD-40, was originally developed to displace
water, which is where its name comes from,

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:56.629
to protect the metal tanks of the Atlas rockets
from rusting, because they weren’t painted

00:00:56.629 --> 00:00:57.629
to save weight.

00:00:57.629 --> 00:00:58.629
[4]

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:00.830
And those Atlas rockets were made of stainless
steel.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:05.280
In those days aluminium alloying material
science hadn’t quite developed far enough,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.020
and the engineers of the Atlas rockets instead
opted to use extremely thin stainless steel

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:15.060
for their propellant tanks, varying from 2.5
millimetres to about 10 millimetres.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.119
These were essentially metal balloons.

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:20.030
As they were structurally unstable when unpressurised.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:26.909
In one infamous case on May 11th 1963, an
Atlas Agena D lost pressurisation on the launch

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:31.780
pad, allowing the weight of the upper stage
to buckle the thin steel.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.540
Pressurisation adds strength to pressure vessels
as the pressure provides a restoring force

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:41.149
for small deformations, so if the metal attempts
to bend inwards the internal pressure pushes

00:01:41.149 --> 00:01:42.570
it back out.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:46.950
This strengthens all rocket tanks allowing
their thickness to be minimised, but this

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.890
application took it to the extreme to make
up for steels density.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:55.790
Our choice of material for aviation and aerospace
applications has evolved with our mastery

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.740
of material science.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.030
Specifically with the materials available
to us that have the highest strength to weight

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.420
ratios.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.729
We can visualise these strength to weight
ratios on graph like this.

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:10.640
Plotting the strength of the material against
its density.[6]

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.850
Looking at this it’s pretty clear that steel
adds a significant amount of weight, while

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:18.130
not adding a proportional amount of strength.

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:23.940
Steel is typically 2.5 times heavier than
aluminium, but it is not 2.5 times stronger.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.540
So why use stainless steel?

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:30.370
Well, strength to weight ratios are not the
only factor engineers have to consider.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.070
Something you may not consider are things
like thermal conductivity.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.610
Aluminium has a much higher thermal conductivity
than steel, and thus can conduct heat from

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:42.100
its surroundings into the cryogenic fuel much
faster.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:47.300
This can vaporise the fuel, which requires
boil-off valves to vent the vaporised fuel.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:51.710
To minimise this problem, rocket fuel tanks
are often sprayed with foam insulation, that’s

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:56.260
what gave the external tank of the space shuttle
it’s distinctive orange colour, but this

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.990
adds a substantial amount of mass itself,
which in turn decreases the weight saving

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:02.770
benefits aluminium provides.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:03.770
[2]

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.430
However, the Falcon-9 fuel tanks are not insulated.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:11.120
To prevent major boil off of the fuel, the
fuel is loaded as late as possible.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.570
This reduces the amount of fuel that will
be vaporised, but also makes the job of getting

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:19.330
the Falcon 9 certified for human payloads
a bit of a nightmare.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:25.190
NASA did not want Space X to fuel the rocket
with passengers on board, because as we saw

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.280
earlier things can go wrong during this phase.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.970
In August 2018, they finally approved the
Falcon 9 for this “load and go” style

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.690
of fueling for human flight.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:35.690
[3]

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:40.200
The aluminium-lithium alloys used in the Falcon-9
were not developed until the late 50s and

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.830
early 60s, which increased their strength
to weight ratios, allowing the introduction

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.140
to aerospace applications.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.140
[4]

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:51.310
The stainless steel balloon tanks of the Atlas
rockets were eventually made with this aluminium

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:55.850
alloy metal, and their strength to weight
ratio were boosted by using a unique stringer

00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:00.870
pattern called an isogrid, which boosted the
aluminiums ability to resist buckling, like

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:02.830
that of the Atlas Agena D.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:07.150
NASA performed these huge compressive buckling
tests on the aluminium lithium tanks of the

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.180
SLS rocket.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.160
Typically you use little strain gauges, whos
electrical resistance change as you stretch

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.500
them forcing the electrons along a longer
path to keep track of the strain in the material,

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:22.000
but for something this big they would have
needed thousands.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.220
Instead they painted dots all over the structure
to allow computer imaging software to keep

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:27.980
track of the strain.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:33.480
That isogrid structure is excellent for maximising
strength while minimising the material needed.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.730
It is essentially an inter woven pattern of
I beams that increase the stiffness of the

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:38.880
overall structure.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.970
You will see this pattern everywhere in aerospace.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:46.350
From these sixties era rockets to Space X's
new dragon 2 capsule.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:50.860
Space X, to date, has used aluminium-lithium
alloys in their propellant tanks.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:55.440
But they opted not to use this isogrid structure,
even though it provides fantastic strength

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.330
to weight performance, it is absurdly expensive
to manufacture.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:03.610
To manufacture isogrids you start off with
a thicker piece of aluminium and machine it

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.270
down using a CNC machine.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:10.620
This results in about 95% of the material
going to waste.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.910
Instead Spacex opted for a thin skin of aluminium-lithium
alloy and then stir welded strengthening stringers

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:18.220
in place.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.730
We are constantly balancing a huge number
of factors.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:25.800
Here the cost of manufacturing the rocket
influenced it’s design.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.710
Typically the cost of launching an extra kilogram
of material to space far outweighs the cost

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:36.210
of material, but in cases like this the waste
in the manufacturing process can influence

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:37.620
our material choice.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.040
For example Musk attributed the cost of carbon
fibre composites as one of the primary reasons

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:45.410
he abandoned it as a material for the Starhopper.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:50.880
Carbon fibre composites cost about 135 dollars
per kilogram, and a significant amount of

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:53.430
it is thrown away in the lay-up process.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:58.480
The manufacturing process for carbon fibre
composites is extraordinarily expensive and

00:05:58.480 --> 00:05:59.480
difficult.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.570
As explained in my carbon fibre video.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:05.450
Carbon fibre composites gain all of their
strength from the long and thin carbon fibres

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.660
inside the plastic resin that holds them together.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:13.389
This means that their strength is not the
same in all directions, and in order to ensure

00:06:13.389 --> 00:06:18.070
the material can be strong in all directions
you have to layer your carbon fibre composite

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:19.920
in a very specific way.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.310
You then have to cure it in a pressurised
oven.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:26.480
This was one of the major flaws I pointed
out in predicting the failure of the early

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.889
prototypes of the BFR carbon composite tanks,
which were made in two parts presumably because

00:06:31.889 --> 00:06:37.370
they couldn’t find tooling and an autoclave
big enough to cure a full sized tank.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:41.840
Being perfectly honest this is the only subject
area where I have enough expertise to make

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.221
comments on other peoples designs, and I was
surprised Space X were pursuing the material

00:06:46.221 --> 00:06:51.070
at all, for the reasons stated above, and
as it’s unsuitable for a vehicle that not

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:55.480
only has to withstand the freezing temperatures
from the cryogenic fuel on assent, but the

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.850
scorching temperatures of re-entry.

00:06:57.850 --> 00:06:59.490
Not once, but twice.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:04.600
As this will be the first vehicle in history
expected to visit Mars AND return.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:09.230
Here we really start to see where stainless
steel shines, and why Musk is opting for a

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:10.230
stainless steel vehicle.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:15.520
Let’s plot another graph, this time plotting
strength against maximum operating temperature.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.400
Here we can see that stainless steel outperforms
both aluminium alloys and carbon fibre composites

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.060
by a significant margin.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:23.060
[6]

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:27.560
The Falcon 9 first stage rocket serves only
to boost the second stage to about 65 to 75

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:34.300
km in altitude and between 6,000 to 8,300
km/h, before flipping over and performing

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:39.270
re-entry burns to slow down before entering
the thicker atmosphere at relatively slow

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:40.280
speeds.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:44.670
Even then, the engine nozzles, which are designed
to tolerate massive temperatures take the

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:49.490
brunt of the re-entry heating, allowing the
aluminium tanks to avoid any major reentry

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:50.490
heat.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:55.560
This is not how the Starhopper is intended
to work, because it is being built as an interplanetary

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:56.560
vehicle.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:01.570
The starhopper can expect to enter into the
Martian atmosphere at speeds of up to 21,000

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:06.460
km/h and experience temperatures up to 1,700
degrees.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:10.710
Well above the maximum service temperature
of both aluminium and stainless steel, but

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.740
we have ways of leaching some of that heat
away before it can heat the metal.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:20.060
The curiosity rover utilised a phenolic impregnated
carbon ablator, which is extremely extremely

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:25.640
light, has a low thermal conductivity, and
can resist extreme temperatures of up to 1,930

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:26.640
degrees.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:27.640
[5]

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:31.381
But nothing this heavy has ever entered the
Martian atmosphere before, and it’s not

00:08:31.381 --> 00:08:33.750
going to be any easy task for it to slow it
down.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:38.719
It’s going to have to enter the martian
atmosphere at an extremely high angle of attack

00:08:38.719 --> 00:08:44.060
to allow the thin martian atmosphere to sap
away speed through drag for an extended period,

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:46.120
but drag comes with heat.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:50.190
Stainless steel may be heavy, but it will
require significantly less heat shielding

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.980
that an aluminium or carbon fibre composites.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:58.170
Once again closing that weight advantage gap
of these alternate materials . In fact Musk

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:02.891
has stated that the rear side of the Starhopper
will require no heat shielding at all, and

00:09:02.891 --> 00:09:07.170
he plans to use a strange technique to cool
the wind facing side of the vehicle.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.590
Using the same method humans use to cool down,
by sweating.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:15.260
Musk plans to pump liquid methane between
two steel panels on the windward facing side

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:20.300
of the Space X rocket, where it will gain
heat, vaporise and evaporate through small

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.520
holes in the rockets surface.

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:26.740
This is pretty weird way of cooling a ship,
and I wondered why you would not just opt

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:30.180
to use the tried and true method of ablative
tiles.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:34.410
Then I remembered that this ship needs to
make a return journey, and the entry into

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:38.470
the Martian atmosphere will damage the tiles
and require maintenance.

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:43.270
There is no oil on mars to manufacture new
phenolic resin or the carbon needed for ablatives.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:48.890
So, using methane, the fuel the new Raptor
engines that Space X will use for the Starhopper,

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:50.020
makes a lot sense.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:55.360
It reduces the equipment the rocket will need
to carry to Mars, making the rocket significantly

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:56.360
lighter.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:01.420
They can just use the equipment they already
needed for refueling, making it double purpose.

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:06.000
They just need to mine water and extract carbon
dioxide from the atmosphere, and then do some

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.010
fancy chemistry to produce methane and oxygen.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:13.210
The prototype they are building at the moment
is likely just to test the manufacturing techniques

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:16.580
needed to build it, and test it’s flight
capabilities.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:20.490
This ship does not need to be space worthy,
it just needs to have the same weight, centre

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:24.080
of gravity and shape to allow space x to test
it.

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:28.380
On the surface though the whole operation
looks like a bit of a shitshow, and I really

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:33.221
try to be positive about engineering advancements,
but the thing literally fell over in the wind

00:10:33.221 --> 00:10:34.221
last week.

00:10:34.221 --> 00:10:37.930
I’m really curious on how this whole thing
is going to unfold.

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:41.800
Sometimes you just need to make mistakes to
learn, which is why you should sign up to

